 ## Background
-The concept of feature fields originates from the observation that Neural Radiance Fields are not limited to learning RGB colors; they can learn any quantity that can be projected and supervised from 2D observations [S1, unofficial]. Semantic-NeRF [207] was notably the first to introduce semantic fields within the NeRF framework, using ground-truth semantic segmentation maps to augment the radiance field with per-point semantic logits [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent works have expanded this to include object codes [S2, unofficial], self-supervised segmentation via teacher networks like DINO [S3], and geometric priors for 3D detection [S4].
+Feature Fields were introduced as a generalization of the NeRF formulation, allowing the prediction of arbitrary per-point feature signals rather than only color [S1, unofficial]. Semantic-NeRF was notably the first to introduce semantic fields within the NeRF framework, using ground-truth semantic segmentation maps to augment the radiance field with per-point semantic logits, enabling dense 3D semantic understanding [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent works have expanded this concept to include object codes [S2, unofficial], self-supervised segmentation features [S3], and geometric priors for 3D detection [S4].
 
